{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '450,610p' docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md; cat src/cljc/dao/data/btree/storage.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"timeout.\n\nFor an integrated work operation, observe first, then charge, then\nperform the operation only if the state remains live. Perform the\nrequired post-operation observation before interpreting or delivering its\nresult. An existing terminal state is absorbing: subsequent operations\nreturn that same state, perform no work and take no clock observations.\n\nThe last available work unit may execute. Completion with no remaining\nunits succeeds only when no further work is required and the final\ndeadline observation is live. A value returned by a read whose post-read\nobservation expires must not be interpreted or delivered.\n\n### Forcing discipline\n\nConstructing a lazy sequence performs no accountable evaluation by\nitself. The driver must control the forced pass, including source\ntraversal, candidate generation, projection, deduplication, aggregation,\npull and final materialization.\n\nDo not place the only checks around `q`, `collect`, a clause, or the\nconstruction of a range sequence. Chunked realization must not perform an\nunchecked batch of work.\n\nPlanning must continue to avoid forcing deferred source rows merely to\nestimate clause cost.\n\nA completed result must retain no deferred work capable of reading a\nborrowed source after return. Intermediate lazy values must not escape\nwith reusable stale budget state.\n\nThe Phase 4a implementation is authorized to rewrite the evaluator into\nstrict state-passing operations. Strict state passing does not require\nexhaustive materialization of every intermediate relation. The common\nkernel must preserve first-witness termination for existential evaluation\nsuch as `not` and `not-join`, using explicit demand, checked folds or\nexecution frames.\n\nQuery-controlled loops must return progress, including loops that reject\nevery candidate or produce no binding. Lazy evaluator chains that capture\nstale execution state are not permitted. Adapter preprocessing and final\noutput work participate in the same invocation state.\n\nExisting selective index access and planning-never-forces behavior remain\nregression requirements for unbounded calls. This rewrite does not\nauthorize an eager source drain or a second query semantics\nimplementation.\n\nPhase 4a must make query-controlled, potentially unbounded evaluation\nchains stack-safe through a trampoline or explicit execution frames. This\nincludes recursive rule expansion and continuation chains whose depth\ngrows with processed bindings or nested evaluation. Increasing the work\nallowance must not replace structured exhaustion with host stack\noverflow. A fixed rule-depth guard is not a substitute for this\nrequirement. Phase 3 tabling addresses recursive completeness and must\npreserve this execution property. Opaque caller functions retain the\nseparately documented indivisible-operation limitation.\n\n### Storage checkpoints and overshoot\n\nThe required storage-work guarantee is:\n\n**Once a deadline passes during a storage read, expiry is observed when\nthat read returns and before another query-controlled storage read starts.\nAt most the one in-flight storage read is allowed beyond the deadline.**\n\nCheckpoint before and after each actual node fetch, including fetches\ninside a lazy range traversal. Also checkpoint around range entry,\nadvancement and exit.\n\nChecks only before and after `select-datoms` or a whole range scan cannot\nestablish this guarantee: one scan may fault many B-tree nodes. The\nbaseline range-selection seam at `query.cljc:1042–1044` therefore needs\nchecked traversal down to each actual read. Use generic explicit read\nsteps or an equivalent invocation-scoped traversal mechanism; do not\nmutate a borrowed shared tree to install query state.\n\nThis is an operation-count bound, not a millisecond upper bound. A single\nsynchronous backend read may block beyond the deadline, and so may a\nhost function. The adapter cannot interrupt such a call. On return it\nchecks before further work. CPU-only work has the corresponding\nnext-checkpoint guarantee, subject to the documented indivisible\noperation.\n\nQualification must demonstrate the one-read guarantee on the supported\npath. If the existing lazy traversal cannot expose that granularity,\nimplement the generic seam or explicitly leave the timed path\nunqualified. Do not replace the guarantee with checks at clause boundaries.\n\n### Staged source qualification: Phase 4a and Phase 4b\n\nThe one-in-flight-storage-read guarantee is unchanged. Phase 4a does not\nclaim to establish it for published indexes.\n\nPhase 4a may qualify bounded queries over explicitly materialized\nrelation and fact-relation values, and supported views whose complete\nsource chain terminates in such a materialized value. Their\nquery-controlled realization, current-state interpretation, index\nconstruction and evaluation must participate in work accounting and\ndeadline observations.\n\nCalls supplying either a timeout or a work cap must refuse a source\nchain reaching an opened published index, deferred source rows or another\nunqualified source implementation. Admission inspects descriptors without\nforcing source contents and checks every declared source before source\nrealization. Cache residency does not change eligibility.\n\nRefusal uses the adapter's `:unsupported` category, a minimal\n`:bounded-source-unqualified` reason and the source variable. It must\nperform zero source reads and must not open or close anything. The\nadapter must not silently materialize a rejected source or fall back to\nunchecked execution.\n\nUnbounded calls retain existing source support through the common\nevaluator. Explicit materialization performed by the caller before\ninvoking `q` is outside that query's bounds and must be described as\nsuch.\n\nPhase 4b owns the checked published-index traversal seam. Its design must\ncover root restoration, initial seek descent, range advancement,\nfull-walk fallback and reads made by pull or special forms. It must\nreturn invocation progress or explicit read requests at each actual\nfetch, without mutating a borrowed tree to install query state and\nwithout making storage interpret query semantics.\n\nA wrapping `IStorage` alone is not sufficient evidence: its design must\nexplain how immutable progress returns from each restore. The Phase 4b\nseam requires architecture review before implementation/landing. Phase 5\ncannot qualify bounded published-index queries until Phase 4b passes.\n\n| Source path | Unbounded calls | Bounded calls after Phase 4a | Bounded calls after Phase 4b |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Materialized relation/fact-relation | Existing supported semantics | Eligible for qualification after integrated tests | Same |\n| Supported view over materialized relation | Existing supported semantics | Eligible after checked realization and evaluation tests | Same |\n| Published index, directly or under a view | Existing supported semantics | Refused before source reads | Eligible only for paths proven by checked-traversal tests |\n| Deferred or unrecognized source implementation | No new support granted | Refused | Remains refused unless separately qualified |\n\n\"Eligible\" does not mean tests or Client oracle verification have already\npassed.\n\n### Exhaustion and exceptions\n\nThe generic engine may represent exhaustion as a terminal data outcome.\nThe public compatibility adapter raises structured `ex-info`; it never\nreturns a partial successful relation.\n\nThis deliberately differs from repository mechanisms that report\nexhaustion as `:dao.stream/blocked`, parked state, or `:lost`/`:deadline`.\nIt is not an invariant breach: the query API is an interpreter boundary\nwith an exception contract. Streams retain their own data-outcome\nsemantics, and the adapter neither rewrites nor consumes their history\ndestructively.\n\nThe design can compose with a future stepped remote driver, but this\nprofile adds no query RPC or remote-execution protocol.\n\n### Deterministic testing\n\nSemantic tests inject a fake driver that expires after a specified number\nof observations. Test immediately-before, exact-boundary and\nimmediately-after outcomes, plus work exhaustion independently of time.\n(ns dao.data.btree.storage\n  \"IStorage adapters over dao.jing content-store handles (docs/design/dao.data.btree.md\n   sections 5.1, 5.4). A handle is the plain-data byte store carrying\n   :put-bytes-fn, :get-bytes-fn, and :close-fn (docs/design/dao.jing.md,\n   Materialization rule); addresses are derived from payloads by\n   dao.jing/materialize!, never supplied by this layer. Two adapters, one\n   error taxonomy:\n\n   - KVStorage (kv-storage): the sync rule-1 adapter. Absence is\n     authoritative: a missing blob throws \\\"missing index segment\\\".\n     Optional same-host integrity verification (§5.2): rehash the fetched\n     blob against its content address; mismatch throws \\\"corrupt index\n     segment\\\". Off by default — dao.jing's print-based content hash is not\n     host-stable, so cross-host reads must not verify (dao.jing.md, Current\n     Scope).\n   - HydrationStorage (hydration-storage): the §5.4 hydration-cache\n     adapter for async-only backends. Reads answer only from the cache;\n     any miss throws \\\"unhydrated segment\\\" — the cache cannot distinguish\n     absent from not-yet-fetched without blocking on the backend. hydrate!\n     copies the reachable blob graph from the source into the cache. The\n     source is either a sync content handle or an async one\n     (dao.jing.content.async/async-content); over an async source the\n     non-blocking hydrate-async and store-tree-async are the only way in\n     and out, and the sync store path throws.\n\n   dao.data.btree itself stays storage-agnostic; this namespace is the one\n   place the tree meets dao.jing (Decision 1: no new storage protocol —\n   everything below is materialize!/get, plus segment-matches? for §5.2\n   verification).\"\n  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:async\" :as async]])\n            [dao.data.btree :as bt]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]))\n\n\n(def ^:private absent\n  \"Identity sentinel for handle misses (§5.1 sketch): keywords are not\n   reliably `identical?` on cljs, an opaque host object is.\"\n  #?(:cljd (Object.)\n     :clj (Object.)\n     :cljs (js-obj)))\n\n\n(deftype KVStorage\n  [store settings verify? algorithm]\n\n  bt/IStorage\n\n  (-store\n    [_ node]\n    (let [blob (bt/node->blob node)]\n      (if algorithm\n        (jing/materialize! store blob {:algorithm algorithm})\n        (jing/materialize! store blob))))\n\n\n  (-restore\n    [_ addr]\n    (let [blob (jing/get store addr absent)]\n      (when (identical? blob absent)\n        (throw (ex-info \"missing index segment\" {:address addr})))\n      (when verify?\n        (when-not (jing/segment-matches? addr blob)\n          (throw (ex-info \"corrupt index segment\"\n                          {:expected addr, :actual (jing/segment-key blob)}))))\n      (bt/blob->node blob settings)))\n\n\n  (-accessed [_ _addr] nil)\n\n\n  (-settings [_] settings))\n\n\n(defn kv-storage\n  \"A sync IStorage over a dao.jing content-store handle. opts:\n   {:branching-factor n (default 512) :ref-type k (default per host, §5.3)\n   :verify? bool (default false, §5.2 — same-host mint+read only)\n   :algorithm keyword (default nil, mints with default-hash-algorithm)}. The\n   returned storage owns the Settings every tree restored through it shares\n   (§5.1 threading rule); pass the manifest's :branching-factor here.\"\n  ([store] (kv-storage store nil))\n  ([store opts]\n   (let [box (volatile! nil)\n         sett (bt/->Settings (or (:branching-factor opts) 512)\n                             (or (:ref-type opts) (bt/default-ref-type*))\n                             nil\n                             box)\n         storage (KVStorage. store\n                             sett\n                             (boolean (:verify? opts))\n                             (:algorithm opts))]\n     (vreset! box storage)\n     storage)))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Hydration cache (§5.4)\n\n(defn- async-source?\n  \"True for an async content handle (dao.jing.content.async/async-content).\"\n  [source]\n  (some? (:get-content-async-fn source)))\n\n\n(deftype HydrationStorage\n  [source cache settings outbox algorithm]\n  ;; outbox: atom {:writing? bool, :unacked [[addr blob] ...]} — used\n  ;; only over an async source. :writing? is true only inside\n  ;; store-tree-async; :unacked holds every blob stored to the cache whose\n  ;; source write has not yet acknowledged, carried across failed calls so\n  ;; a retry re-pushes them (§5.4 durability ordering).\n\n  bt/IStorage\n\n  (-store\n    [_ node]\n    (let [blob (bt/node->blob node)\n          opts (when algorithm {:algorithm algorithm})]\n      (if (async-source? source)\n        (do\n          ;; the sync store-tree cannot wait for acknowledgments (§5.4)\n          (when-not (:writing? @outbox)\n            (throw (ex-info \"sync store-tree against an async backend\" {})))\n          (let [addr (if opts\n                       (jing/materialize! cache blob opts)\n                       (jing/materialize! cache blob))]\n            (swap! outbox update :unacked\n                   (fn [u]\n                     (if (some #(= addr (first %)) u) u (conj u [addr blob]))))\n            addr))\n        ;; writes land in the durable source AND the read cache; both are\n        ;; content-addressed, so both must answer with the same address\n        (let [source-addr (if opts\n                            (jing/materialize! source blob opts)\n                            (jing/materialize! source blob))\n              cache-addr (if opts\n                           (jing/materialize! cache blob opts)\n                           (jing/materialize! cache blob))]\n          (when-not (= source-addr cache-addr)\n            (throw (ex-info \"hydration source and cache diverged\"\n                            {:source source-addr, :cache cache-addr})))\n          source-addr))))\n\n\n  (-restore\n    [_ addr]\n    (let [blob (jing/get cache addr absent)]\n      (if (identical? blob absent)\n        ;; absent vs not-hydrated is undecidable synchronously (§5.4)\n        (throw (ex-info \"unhydrated segment\" {:address addr}))\n        (bt/blob->node blob settings))))\n\n\n  (-accessed [_ _addr] nil)\n\n\n  (-settings [_] settings))\n\n\n(defn hydration-storage\n  \"The §5.4 hydration-cache adapter: reads answer only from `cache` (miss\n   => \\\"unhydrated segment\\\"); `hydrate!` fills the cache from `source`.\n   `source` is a sync content handle (hydrate!, store-tree) or an async\n   one from dao.jing.content.async (hydrate-async, store-tree-async).\"\n  ([source cache] (hydration-storage source cache nil))\n  ([source cache opts]\n   (let [box (volatile! nil)\n         sett (bt/->Settings (or (:branching-factor opts) 512)\n                             (or (:ref-type opts) (bt/default-ref-type*))\n                             nil\n                             box)\n         storage (HydrationStorage. source\n                                    cache\n                                    sett\n                                    (atom {:writing? false, :unacked []})\n                                    (:algorithm opts))]\n     (vreset! box storage)\n     storage)))\n\n\n(defn hydrate!\n  \"Full-graph hydration (§5.4 rule 2, blocking variant): copy every blob\n   reachable from the set's root address out of the source backend into\n   the hydration cache, so subsequent reads — and mutations, whose write\n   path requires residency — succeed. Idempotent: already-cached segments\n   are re-materialized as no-ops. Returns the set. On a non-hydration\n   storage this is a no-op (sync backends need no hydration).\"\n  [s]\n  (let [storage (bt/set-storage s)]\n    (when (instance? HydrationStorage storage)\n      (let [^HydrationStorage hs storage\n            source (.-source hs)\n            cache (.-cache hs)]\n        (when (async-source? source)\n          (throw (ex-info \"hydrate! against an async backend; use hydrate-async\"\n                          {})))\n        (letfn [(pull!\n                  [addr]\n                  (let [blob (jing/get source addr absent)]\n                    (when (identical? blob absent)\n                      (throw (ex-info \"missing index segment\" {:address addr})))\n                    (let [algo (jing/segment-algorithm addr)\n                          addr' (jing/materialize! cache blob\n                                                   {:algorithm algo})]\n                      (when-not (= addr addr')\n                        (throw (ex-info \"hydration address mismatch\"\n                                        {:expected addr, :actual addr'}))))\n                    (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (pull! a))))]\n          (when-some [addr (bt/set-address s)] (pull! addr)))))\n    s))\n\n\n;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Async variants (§5.4 rule 2 non-blocking, and durability ordering)\n\n(defn- deferred\n  \"The host's one-shot async value: a Promise (cljs), a Completer-backed\n   Future (cljd), a CompletableFuture (JVM). `start` receives resolve and\n   reject fns and calls exactly one of them.\"\n  [start]\n  #?(:cljd (let [c (async/Completer)]\n             (start #(.complete c %) #(.completeError c %))\n             (.-future c))\n     :clj (let [f (java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture.)]\n            (start #(.complete f %) #(.completeExceptionally f %))\n            f)\n     :cljs (js/Promise. (fn [resolve reject] (start resolve reject)))))\n\n\n(defn- attempt\n  \"Run thunk `f`, answering [:ok v] or [:err e] — so a callback is never\n   called from inside the try that guards the work it reports on.\"\n  [f]\n  (try [:ok (f)]\n       (catch #?(:cljd Object\n                 :clj Throwable\n                 :cljs :default)\n              e\n         [:err e])))\n\n\n(defn- report\n  [[tag x] on-ok on-err]\n  (if (= :ok tag) (on-ok x) (on-err x)))\n\n\n(defn- async-hydration-storage\n  \"The storage when it is a HydrationStorage over an async source, else nil.\"\n  [storage]\n  (when (and (instance? HydrationStorage storage)\n             (async-source? (.-source ^HydrationStorage storage)))\n    storage))\n\n\n(defn hydrate-async\n  \"Full-graph hydration (§5.4 rule 2, non-blocking variant): fetch every\n   blob reachable from the set's root address out of the async source into\n   the hydration cache, then resolve to the same set. Addresses already in\n   the cache are read from it rather than fetched, so hydrating a resident\n   graph issues no requests. Over a sync source it runs hydrate!; over a\n   non-hydration storage it resolves at once.\n\n   (hydrate-async s)              => Promise / Future / CompletableFuture\n   (hydrate-async s on-ok on-err) => nil; exactly one callback is called\n\n   A source miss rejects \\\"missing index segment\\\" (the source answers\n   absence authoritatively); an `:error` or `:lost` completion rejects\n   \\\"hydration fetch failed\\\" with the completion attached.\"\n  ([s] (deferred (fn [resolve reject] (hydrate-async s resolve reject))))\n  ([s on-ok on-err]\n   (if-let [^HydrationStorage hs (async-hydration-storage (bt/set-storage s))]\n     (let [cache (.-cache hs)\n           get-async (:get-content-async-fn (.-source hs))\n           settled (atom false)\n           ok! #(when (compare-and-set! settled false true) (on-ok s))\n           fail! #(when (compare-and-set! settled false true) (on-err %))\n           ;; one token for the walk itself plus one per visited address\n           outstanding (atom 1)\n           seen (atom #{})\n           done! #(when (zero? (swap! outstanding dec)) (ok!))]\n       (letfn [(visit!\n                 [addr]\n                 (when-not (contains? (first (swap-vals! seen conj addr)) addr)\n                   (swap! outstanding inc)\n                   (let [blob (jing/get cache addr absent)]\n                     (if (identical? blob absent)\n                       (get-async addr #(report (attempt (fn [] (fetched! addr %)))\n                                                (fn [_] nil)\n                                                fail!))\n                       (do (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (visit! a))\n                           (done!))))))\n               (fetched!\n                 [addr c]\n                 (cond\n                   (not (contains? c :found?))\n                   (fail! (ex-info \"hydration fetch failed\"\n                                   {:address addr, :completion c}))\n\n                   (not (:found? c))\n                   (fail! (ex-info \"missing index segment\" {:address addr}))\n\n                   :else\n                   (let [blob (:value c)\n                         algo (jing/segment-algorithm addr)\n                         addr' (jing/materialize! cache blob\n                                                  {:algorithm algo})]\n                     (if (= addr addr')\n                       (do (doseq [a (:addresses blob)] (visit! a))\n                           (done!))\n                       (fail! (ex-info \"hydration address mismatch\"\n                                       {:expected addr, :actual addr'}))))))]\n         (report (attempt (fn []\n                            (when-some [addr (bt/set-address s)] (visit! addr))\n                            (done!)))\n                 (fn [_] nil)\n                 fail!))\n       nil)\n     (report (attempt #(hydrate! s)) on-ok on-err))))\n\n\n(defn store-tree-async\n  \"The write-side sibling of hydrate-async (§5.4 durability ordering):\n   store the set's dirty subgraph into the hydration cache, push every\n   unacknowledged segment to the async source, and resolve to the root\n   address only after every one of those writes has acknowledged. The\n   caller chains the root `cas!` on that resolution — never before.\n\n   A segment whose write fails stays queued on the storage and is pushed\n   again by the next store-tree-async through it, so retrying a failed\n   call — even on the now-addressed set, whose store-tree is a no-op —\n   cannot resolve over an incomplete closure. Over a sync storage it\n   resolves to store-tree's result. An empty set resolves to nil, as\n   store-tree answers.\n\n   (store-tree-async s storage)              => Promise / Future / CompletableFuture\n   (store-tree-async s storage on-ok on-err) => nil; exactly one callback is called\"\n  ([s storage]\n   (deferred (fn [resolve reject] (store-tree-async s storage resolve reject))))\n  ([s storage on-ok on-err]\n   (if-let [^HydrationStorage hs (async-hydration-storage storage)]\n     (let [outbox (.-outbox hs)\n           source (.-source hs)\n           put-content-async (or (:put-content-async-fn source)\n                                 (when-let [m (:materialize-async-fn source)]\n                                   (fn [addr blob cb]\n                                     (m blob\n                                        {:algorithm\n                                         (jing/segment-algorithm addr)}\n                                        cb))))\n           stored (attempt (fn []\n                             (swap! outbox assoc :writing? true)\n                             (try (bt/store-tree s storage)\n                                  (finally (swap! outbox assoc :writing? false)))))]\n       (if (= :err (first stored))\n         (report stored on-ok on-err)\n         (let [root (second stored)\n               unacked (:unacked @outbox)\n               remaining (atom (count unacked))\n               failure (atom nil)\n               settle! #(when (zero? (swap! remaining dec))\n                          (if-some [e @failure] (on-err e) (on-ok root)))]\n           (if (empty? unacked)\n             (on-ok root)\n             (doseq [[addr blob] unacked]\n               (put-content-async\n                 addr\n                 blob\n                 (fn [c]\n                   (if (and (:result c) (= addr (:address c)))\n                     ;; acknowledged: the one place a segment leaves the queue\n                     (swap! outbox update :unacked\n                            (fn [u] (filterv #(not= addr (first %)) u)))\n                     (compare-and-set!\n                       failure\n                       nil\n                       (ex-info \"store-tree-async: segment write failed\"\n                                {:address addr, :completion c})))\n                   (settle!)))))))\n       nil)\n     (report (attempt #(bt/store-tree s storage)) on-ok on-err))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat src/cljc/dao/space/query/execution.cljc; rg -n 'gets|counting-content|<=.*(4|15)' test/dao/space/query_test.cljc; sed -n '547,612p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat src/cljc/dao/space/query/execution.cljc; rg -n 'gets|counting-content|<=.*(4|15)' test/dao/space/query_test.cljc; sed -n '547,612p' src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.space.query.execution\n  \"Generic bounded-execution machinery for dao.space query\n   (docs/design/dao.space.query.datomic-compat.md, Normative execution\n   bounds). It names no Client option and no query clause.\n\n   One invocation owns one IMMUTABLE state: progress, remaining work\n   allowance, deadline data, phase. Every operation takes a state and returns\n   a new state; nothing here is an atom, volatile, dynamic var, callback or\n   namespace-level clock. A caller that forks the same state into two\n   branches has not threaded it: each step must receive the state the prior\n   step returned.\n\n   Time enters only through an explicit driver (`IDriver`): a synchronous\n   observation the driver takes when a checkpoint asks for one. The driver is\n   never registered and never stored in the state; the deadline is absolute\n   data in the same unit the driver reports.\n\n   Exhaustion is a terminal DATA outcome, not an exception: once a state\n   is terminal, every later charge, observation and read returns the same\n   outcome without touching the driver (no read after observed expiry). The\n   public adapter translates the outcome to an exception.\n\n   Status (Phase 4a): the state is threaded through the one evaluation\n   kernel in `dao.space.query` (`q-with-state`) and the datomic adapter; legacy\n   `q` runs the same kernel with `unbounded`. Storage reads inside a lazy\n   B-tree range are NOT checkpointed per node fetch: bounded calls are\n   admitted only over materialized sources, and checked published-index\n   traversal is the separately reviewed Phase 4b.\")\n\n\n(defprotocol IDriver\n\n  (-observe\n    [driver]\n    \"One synchronous time observation, a number in the deadline's unit.\"))\n\n\n(defrecord FnDriver\n  [now-fn]\n\n  IDriver\n\n  (-observe [_] (now-fn)))\n\n\n(defn fn-driver\n  \"A driver over a zero-argument now-fn (monotonic where the host offers\n   one; a wall-clock fallback must be documented by the composer).\"\n  [now-fn]\n  (->FnDriver now-fn))\n\n\n(def step-units\n  \"The operation-to-unit table, as data. Work units are logical accounting,\n   not CPU instructions or elapsed time. A candidate step is one attempted\n   candidate-binding production, charged whether it is retained or rejected.\n   The other entries are the explicit units for operations that could\n   otherwise continue without attempting a binding. Opaque host functions,\n   parsing primitives and storage calls are INDIVISIBLE: one unit does not\n   preempt them.\"\n  {:candidate 1\n   :scan-advance 1\n   :rule-entry 1\n   :aggregate-input 1\n   :pull-advance 1\n   :parse-step 1\n   :output-row 1})\n\n\n(def phases\n  \"Phase boundaries observed at entry and exit.\"\n  [:parse :realize :plan :join :nested :recursion :aggregate :pull :output])\n\n\n(defn start\n  \"A fresh invocation state. `:deadline` is absolute data in the driver's\n   unit (nil: none); `:max-steps` is the work allowance (nil: unbounded, no\n   ambient default).\"\n  [{:keys [deadline max-steps]}]\n  (when-not (or (nil? max-steps) (and (integer? max-steps) (pos? max-steps)))\n    (throw (ex-info \"max-steps must be a positive integer\" {:option :max-steps})))\n  {::steps 0\n   ::remaining max-steps\n   ::deadline deadline\n   ::phase nil\n   ::outcome nil})\n\n\n(def unbounded\n  \"The explicit never-expiring policy legacy q composes with.\"\n  (start {}))\n\n\n(defn outcome\n  \"The terminal outcome data, or nil while the state is live.\"\n  [state]\n  (::outcome state))\n\n\n(defn terminal?\n  [state]\n  (some? (::outcome state)))\n\n\n(defn steps\n  [state]\n  (::steps state))\n\n\n(defn phase\n  [state]\n  (::phase state))\n\n\n(defn- terminal-with\n  [state kind]\n  (assoc state ::outcome {:kind kind\n                          :phase (::phase state)\n                          :steps (::steps state)}))\n\n\n(defn charge\n  \"Consume the units of operation `op` (a key of `step-units`, or an\n   integer count). Refuses BEFORE performing the unit when no allowance\n   remains: the state turns terminal :step-budget-exhausted and the unit is\n   not consumed. No clock is read. A terminal state is returned unchanged.\"\n  [state op]\n  (let [n (if (integer? op) op (get step-units op))]\n    (when-not (and (integer? n) (pos? n))\n      (throw (ex-info \"unknown or non-positive step unit\" {:variable (str op)})))\n    (cond\n      (terminal? state) state\n      (and (some? (::remaining state)) (< (::remaining state) n))\n      (terminal-with state :step-budget-exhausted)\n      :else (cond-> (update state ::steps + n)\n              (some? (::remaining state)) (update ::remaining - n)))))\n\n\n(defn enter\n  \"Record a phase without observing the clock (the next step observes). A\n   terminal state is returned unchanged.\"\n  [state phase]\n  (if (terminal? state) state (assoc state ::phase phase)))\n\n\n(defn bounded?\n  \"True when the state carries a deadline or a work allowance.\"\n  [state]\n  (or (some? (::deadline state)) (some? (::remaining state))))\n\n\n(defn- observe\n  \"Observe the deadline without changing the phase. A terminal state is\n   returned as the SAME value and the driver is not touched.\"\n  [state driver]\n  (cond\n    (terminal? state) state\n    (nil? (::deadline state)) state\n    (>= (-observe driver) (::deadline state)) (terminal-with state :timeout)\n    :else state))\n\n\n(defn checkpoint\n  \"Observe the deadline at a named phase boundary. A terminal state is\n   absorbing: it is returned unchanged (equal as a whole value, phase\n   included) and the driver is not touched. Otherwise the phase is recorded\n   and, when a deadline exists, the driver is observed; expiry (observation\n   >= deadline) turns the state terminal :timeout.\"\n  [state driver phase]\n  (if (terminal? state)\n    state\n    (observe (assoc state ::phase phase) driver)))\n\n\n(defn step\n  \"One accountable unit, in the normative order: observe the deadline; if\n   live, charge the unit BEFORE it is performed (refusing when no\n   allowance remains). The caller performs the unit only when the returned\n   state is live, and observes again at its post-operation boundary. A\n   terminal state is returned unchanged, no clock read, no unit consumed.\"\n  [state driver op]\n  (let [state (observe state driver)]\n    (if (terminal? state) state (charge state op))))\n\n\n(defn read-step\n  \"Run ONE query-controlled storage read under the one-in-flight\n   guarantee. Observe before the read (a terminal or expired state starts\n   no read); run `read-fn` (indivisible: it may overshoot the deadline);\n   observe when it returns, before anything else can start another.\n   Returns [state value]. The value is ::not-read when no read started and\n   ::discarded when the post-read observation expired: a value whose\n   post-read observation expired is never delivered.\"\n  [state driver read-fn]\n  (let [state (checkpoint state driver :read)]\n    (if (terminal? state)\n      [state ::not-read]\n      (let [v (read-fn)\n            state (checkpoint state driver :read)]\n        (if (terminal? state)\n          [state ::discarded]\n          [state v])))))\n\n\n(defn run-reads\n  \"Perform `read-fns` in order, each through `read-step`, threading the\n   state. Stops at the first terminal state; later reads never start.\n   Returns [state values].\"\n  [state driver read-fns]\n  (reduce (fn [[st acc] f]\n            (let [[st v] (read-step st driver f)]\n              (if (terminal? st)\n                (reduced [st acc])\n                [st (conj acc v)])))\n          [state []]\n          read-fns))\n\n\n(defn finish\n  \"The final observation at the end of the pass. Exact-boundary completion\n   succeeds only when the state is live after it.\"\n  [state driver]\n  (checkpoint state driver :output))\n785:(defn- counting-content-store\n787:  (let [gets (atom 0)\n791:                                   (swap! gets inc)\n793:     :gets (fn [] @gets)}))\n986:      (let [counter (counting-content-store (:store fx))]\n998:                            total-gets ((:gets counter))]\n1000:                        ;; Total gets includes: 1 (manifest) + seek path to\n1002:                        ;; elements, tree height is <= 2. Max gets <= 4.\n1005:                        (is (<= total-gets 4)\n1007:                                 total-gets\n1008:                                 \" gets, strictly bounded <= 4\"))\n1009:                        (is (< total-gets 10)\n1010:                            \"strictly fewer gets than reading full index\")\n1028:      (let [counter (counting-content-store (:store fx))]\n1045:                   total-gets ((:gets counter))]\n1050:                 (<= total-gets 15)\n1053:                   total-gets\n1054:                   \" gets, bounded <= 15: the planner must not drain the source\"))\n1056:                 (< total-gets 50)\n1057:                 \"a full drain of this ~135-node tree would exceed 50 gets\")\n1225:      (let [counter (counting-content-store (:store fx))]\n1232:               (is (= 1 ((:gets counter)))\n(defn- realize-base-st\n  \"Realize a non-view db-value: a relation (and, for a fact relation, its\n   index) or an opened published index.\"\n  [st ctx db-value]\n  (cond\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/relation)\n    (let [tuples (:dao.space.query/relation db-value)\n          fact? (:fact? db-value)\n          st (spend-n st ctx :scan-advance (if fact? (relation-size tuples) 0))]\n      (if (exec/terminal? st)\n        [st nil]\n        (let [fact-index (when fact? (relation->fact-index tuples))\n              st (observe-st st ctx)]\n          (if (exec/terminal? st)\n            [st nil]\n            [st {::relation tuples, ::fact-index fact-index}]))))\n\n    (contains? db-value :dao.space.query/published)\n    (do (when (exec/bounded? st)\n          (throw (ex-info \"bounded execution does not support opened published indexes\"\n                          {:reason :bounded-source-unqualified})))\n        (let [indexes (index/covered-indexes db-value)]\n          [st {::relation (:rows db-value),\n               ::indexes indexes,\n               ::fact-index nil}]))\n\n    :else\n    (throw (ex-info raw-source-message {:input db-value}))))\n\n\n(defn- realize-st\n  \"The checked realization of one db-value: [state realized]. Iterative: the\n   view chain is walked down to its base source in a loop (one accounted\n   unit per descriptor hop) and the views are applied back up in a second\n   loop, so a source chain of any depth uses constant host stack. Tuples and\n   index entries are charged before the (indivisible) host construction and\n   the state is observed when it returns. A bounded state refuses an opened\n   published index: its traversal is not checkpointed.\"\n  [st ctx db-value]\n  (loop [st st\n         v db-value\n         views ()]\n    (when-not (map? v)\n      (throw (ex-info raw-source-message {:input v})))\n    (cond\n      (exec/terminal? st) [st nil]\n\n      (and (contains? v :dao.space.query/view)\n           (not (contains? v :dao.space.query/relation))\n           (not (contains? v :dao.space.query/published)))\n      (recur (spend st ctx :scan-advance) (:source v) (conj views v))\n\n      :else\n      (let [[st base] (realize-base-st st ctx v)]\n        ;; views holds the innermost first: apply back up\n        (loop [st st\n               realized base\n               views views]\n          (cond\n            (exec/terminal? st) [st nil]\n            (empty? views) [st realized]\n            :else (let [[st r] (apply-view-st st ctx (first views) realized)]\n                    (recur st r (rest views)))))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
